await $`git switch -q feat/test-gaps`;
const goldenPrompt = `You are working in the Rust crate musicrs (MUSIC direction-of-arrival estimation, a port of pyMUSIC). Read CLAUDE.md first and follow it, including the workflow rules (tests must pass, cargo fmt, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings; tests > ~10 s get #[ignore = "slow"]).

You are in your own git worktree. First run: git fetch origin && git switch -c feat/golden-cases origin/feat/test-gaps
(feat/test-gaps is open PR #9; it already has golden infrastructure: tests/golden_test.rs with a check() helper, tests/golden/generate_golden.py with CASES and COMPOSITE_CASES, tests/common/mod.rs with wav_path/read_wav_4ch/ErrorLog.)

Two tasks, one commit each (commit messages end with a blank line then "Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"). Do NOT push and do NOT open a PR.

TASK 1 — wider pyMUSIC golden set.
Add more pyMUSIC reference cases via tests/golden/generate_golden.py and wire them into tests/golden_test.rs: all five angles (0, 40, 80, 120, 160 deg) for one or two frequencies below 8 kHz, both durations (1.0s and 2.0s) represented, and one more composite (e.g. mid/high tones such as 3600+4700+5800 Hz at 120 deg) for multi-bin IFB. Keep only frequencies < 8000 Hz (musicrs deliberately differs from pyMUSIC at/above Nyquist). Don't add so many that the golden test exceeds a few seconds in a debug build.
Regenerate with the command in the script's docstring. pyMUSIC and pySoundToolbox are already cloned at:
  ${SP}/ext/pyMUSIC
  ${SP}/ext/pySoundToolbox
e.g. uv run --python 3.10 --with 'numpy==1.23.5' python -I tests/golden/generate_golden.py ${SP}/ext/pyMUSIC ${SP}/ext/pySoundToolbox
Check that existing CSVs regenerate byte-for-byte (git diff shows no change to them). Report the max relative error per new case (cargo test --test golden_test -- --nocapture).

TASK 2 — snapshot musicrs's own output at and above Nyquist.
musicrs handles f == sample_rate/2 (real steering vector) and f > Nyquist (conjugated steering vector) differently from pyMUSIC, on purpose (see CLAUDE.md "Sign convention"), so those cases cannot be compared with pyMUSIC. Pin musicrs's own narrowband spectra so this behaviour cannot change silently:
 - pick a few recordings at 8000 Hz and 9100 Hz (different angles) from tests/data/single-source/;
 - store spectra as CSV in tests/golden/ with a clearly distinct suffix such as *.narrowband.musicrs.csv (same angle_rad,spectrum format), and document in the test file and in CLAUDE.md's Notes that these are musicrs snapshots, not pyMUSIC references;
 - provide a way to regenerate them from Rust, e.g. the test rewrites the files when an env var like MUSICRS_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 is set (document the command in CLAUDE.md);
 - compare with a tight relative tolerance;
 - prove the test is sensitive: temporarily break the above-Nyquist sign flip or the at-Nyquist real vector in src/music.rs steering_vector, confirm the snapshot test fails, then restore the file exactly (git diff src/ must be empty before committing).

Finally run cargo fmt, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo test -- --include-ignored, and confirm everything passes.

Report back: branch name, worktree path, commit hashes, the cases added, the measured errors, how you checked sensitivity, and anything surprising or anything you were unsure about. Keep the report concise.`;
self.goldenAgent = tools.Agent({description: 'Golden cases in worktree', prompt: goldenPrompt, subagent_type: 'general-purpose', isolation: 'worktree', name: 'golden-cases'})
  .then(r => { self.goldenResult = r; log('GOLDEN AGENT DONE'); }, e => { self.goldenResult = e; log('GOLDEN AGENT ERROR', String(e)); });
